What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/.../link_state
Date:		October 2026
Contact:	FuQuan <i@fuquan.moe>
Description:
		Read-only MT6580 firmware PWRAP link diagnostics. Reading the
		attribute performs one serialized, side-effect-free MT6350 DEW
		read test at 0x018c, expecting 0x5aa5. It never forces a reset or retries a failed PMIC command.
		A failed check can queue the bounded online recovery worker.

		init_done and wacs2 report a current raw WACS2 status snapshot.
		source reports kernel if the kernel initialized/rebuilt the link, or
		firmware if the existing setup was retained. firmware_at_probe
		records the original INIT_DONE flag. scan_mask is the 24-bit
		successful sampling window and sample its selected center index;
		both are zero when no scan ran. A rebuild replaces the scan information. stage records the initialization
		stage (firmware for a retained link, complete for kernel init).
		read_test/error report the last test result; checks/failures
		count tests since probe. Counters wrap at 32 bits. reads/writes
		count attempted transactions, including failures and read tests.
		errors/timeouts/init_lost count transport failures, timeout
		failures, and failures with INIT_DONE absent. stale_valid_clears
		counts acknowledgements of VALID seen before a new command;
		it does not prove that every acknowledgement followed a timeout.

		last_error/reg/write/stage/status retain the latest failed
		transport operation even after later successes. Stage 0 waits
		for idle before issuing a command, stage 1 waits for a read
		result, and stage 2 waits for write completion. With no recorded
		failure these fields are zero. A signature mismatch increments
		test failures, not transport errors.

		Counters and the saved failure are snapshotted under a spinlock;
		the live WACS2 status is sampled separately and can change as
		other PMIC users run. The attribute itself never forces destructive recovery;
		control and recovery state are separate attributes below.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/.../recovery_state
Date:		October 2026
Contact:	FuQuan <i@fuquan.moe>
Description:
		Read-only state of MT6580 bounded online PWRAP recovery. Reading
		waits for the SPM/recovery phase mutex and performs no hardware
		transaction. irq is the Linux IRQ, negative when not supplied.
		count/last/raw/wdt/signature/wacs snapshot AP error IRQs. Raw flags
		are generic except watchdog bit0; the remaining layout is not
		claimed as a decoded CRC syndrome.

		pending/recovering/failed indicate queued, running or latched
		failed recovery. attempts/soft/rebuilds/failures/deferred count
		worker attempts, resynchronizations, full serial resets, failures
		and live SPM-phase deferrals. blocked counts rejected public-map
		callbacks; they issue no command and are not transaction errors.
		error is the last recovery result; drain_error is the last full
		rebuild's bounded autonomous-wrapper drain result. A broken bus
		can fail to drain but subsequently reconstruct successfully.

		vproc_snapshot_error and before/after refer to the latest full
		rebuild, not a soft inspection. -ENODATA initially means no full
		rebuild was attempted. Six hex fields are PMIC 0x216/21e/220/222/
		224/230 (control, selectors/actual and transition). A failed before
		read is recorded without claiming a successful comparison.
		Other error fields retain kernel errno. Counters wrap at 32 bits.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/.../recover
Date:		October 2026
Contact:	FuQuan <i@fuquan.moe>
Description:
		Root-writable, asynchronous PWRAP recovery request. Write 1 to
		inspect/resynchronize the link, allowing a necessary full rebuild;
		write 2 to force full two-ended serial reconstruction, bypassing
		the automatic once-per-minute limit. Read recovery_state for the
		result. Invalid values return EINVAL. Live SPM voltage/command
		ownership, shutdown or a system not yet running returns EBUSY.

		A native binding with reset/clock suppliers is required for a full
		rebuild; otherwise that attempt records EOPNOTSUPP. Public clients
		are excluded, saved slots/configuration restored and verified,
		and readable VPROC snapshots compared. No failed original command
		is replayed. A failed recovery remains latched; an administrator
		can explicitly request another attempt after resolving the cause.
